# These five are the whole list. OpenCL-Headers and OpenCL-ICD-Loader were
# vendored here too, for ggml's GPU backend; the app is CPU-only now and both
# are gone. `native/OpenCL-*` left behind by an older checkout can be deleted.
#
# espeak-ng is Kokoro's front end, and it needs *compiled* data tables that its
# own build produces by running the espeak-ng binary — impossible when
# cross-compiling to Android. The tables are staged into assets instead, by
# tools/stage-espeak-data.py, which asserts their format version against the
# constant in this checkout's synthdata.c. Bumping the ref above means
# restaging: espeak refuses to load tables it did not compile.
#
# Kokoro's weights are not vendored at all. They are an ONNX graph downloaded
# through the in-app resolver like any other model, and ONNX Runtime arrives as
# a Maven AAR rather than a source checkout.
#
# quickjs is the workflow Script step's language, and the reason it is here
# rather than a Java-side engine is that it is the only option that gives a
# real language *and* a bound on what it can do. It has a memory limit, an
# interrupt callback that can stop a runaway loop between opcodes, and — the
# part that matters most — its standard library is a separate translation unit.
# Four sources are compiled here: dtoa, libregexp, libunicode and quickjs. The
# fifth, quickjs-libc.c, is where reading files, spawning processes and opening
# sockets live, and leaving it out is not an optimisation. It is the sandbox.
# A script in a workflow gets arithmetic, strings, regular expressions, JSON
# and the outputs of earlier steps, and there is no API surface through which
# it could get anything else.
#
# quickjs-ng rather than Bellard's original: it is the maintained fork, it
# cross-compiles to Android without patching, and its CMake names the four
# sources above as the library, which is what this build lists.
